\begin_layout Section
Algebra
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In mathematics,
 a binary operation is a king of binary function,
 i.e.
 given a pair of input values (operands),
 it produces an output value.
 Some definitions require the two inputs and the output to be elements of the same set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
binary operation
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is a mapping of the elements (pairs) of the cartesian product 
\begin_inset Formula $S\times S$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f:S\times S\to S
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Binary operations are usually written using infix notation such as 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{S}b$
\end_inset

 rather than by functional notation of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $f(a,b)$
\end_inset

,
 thus we also write 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}:S\times S\to S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 be a set equipped with one or several methods of producing elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 from other elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 (operations).
 A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
closed
\emph default
 under these methods if an input of (purely) elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 always results in an element still in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in X$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{S}b\in X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall X\subseteq S$
\end_inset

,
 there is a smallest closed subset 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 (it is the intersection fo all closed subsets that contain 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

).
 Depending on the context,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 is called the closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Given the operation of subtraction 
\begin_inset Formula $-:\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{Q}$
\end_inset

 on the rationals,
 the closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\mathbb{N}}=\mathbb{Z}$
\end_inset

.
 Given the operation of division 
\begin_inset Formula $/:\mathbb{Q}^{*}\times\mathbb{Q}^{*}\to\mathbb{Q}^{*}$
\end_inset

,
 the closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Z}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\mathbb{Z}}^{*}=\mathbb{Q}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
\begin_inset Formula $\forall X\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 closed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}=X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
_
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sets with a single operation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
magma
\emph default
 is set 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 with an operation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bullet_{M}:M\times M\to M:a,b\mapsto a\bullet_{M}b
\]

\end_inset

that 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is closed under:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
closure 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in M$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{M}b\in M$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Minisec
Morphisms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
morphism
\emph default
 of magmas 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:M\to N$
\end_inset

 that preserves the binary operation:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall x,y\in M$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x\bullet_{M}y)=f(x)\bullet_{N}f(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{+}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\cdot$
\end_inset

 is a magma,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $+$
\end_inset

 is also a magma and 
\begin_inset Formula $\log:\mathbb{R}^{+}\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is a moprhism,
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{+}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\log(xy)=\log x+\log y.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Special types of a morphism 
\begin_inset Formula $f:M\to N$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
monomorphism
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is injective.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is an 
\emph on
epimorphism
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is surjective.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is an isomorphism if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is bijective.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition
When 
\begin_inset Formula $M=N$
\end_inset

 specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
a monomorphism is called an 
\emph on
endomorphism
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
an isomorphism is called an 
\emph on
automorphism
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{*}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\cdot$
\end_inset

 is a magma.
 Inversion 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{*}\to\mathbb{R}^{*}:x\mapsto x^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is a automorphism,
 as it is bijective and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{*}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(xy)^{-1}=x^{-1}y^{-1}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
semigroup
\emph default
 is a magma 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 the operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

 of which is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
associative 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b,c\in S$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(a\bullet_{S}b)\bullet_{S}c=a\bullet_{S}(b\bullet_{S}c)=a\bullet_{S}b\bullet_{S}c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
More succinctly,
 a semigroup is an associative magma.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
semigroup congruence
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is an equivalence relation on a semigroup 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 that is compatible with the semigroup operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,a^{\prime},b,b^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a\sim b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}\sim b^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{S}a^{\prime}\sim b\bullet_{S}b^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Like any equivalence relation,
 a semigroup congruence 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 induces congruence classes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in S$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[a]=\{x\in S|x\sim a\},
\]

\end_inset

and the semigroup operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

 induces a binary operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{\sim}$
\end_inset

 on the congruence classes.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in S$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[a]\bullet_{\sim}[b]=[a\bullet_{S}b].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is a congruence,
 the set of all congruence classes of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 forms a semigroup with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{\sim}$
\end_inset

,
 called the 
\emph on
quotient semigroup
\emph default
,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $S/\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
_
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 of a semigroup 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 is a 
\emph on
subsemigroup
\emph default
 iff A is closed under 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{A}=A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A semigroup morphism is called a 
\emph on
homomorphism
\emph default
 specifically.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
monoid
\emph default
 is a semigroup 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 the operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{M}$
\end_inset

 of which (additionally) has
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
identity
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

element 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists!\mathds{1}_{M}\in M$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in S$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{M}\bullet_{M}a=a\bullet_{M}\mathds{1}_{M}=a$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 a monoid is a semigroup with and identity element.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
The identity element of a monoid is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{M},\mathds{1}^{\prime}_{M}\in M$
\end_inset

 be identity elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
_
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
submonoid
\emph default
 of a monoid 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $N\subseteq M$
\end_inset

 that is closed under the monoid operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{M}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{M}\in N$
\end_inset

.
 In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a monoid under the binary operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{M}$
\end_inset

 inherited from 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
monoid homomorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f:M\to N$
\end_inset

 is a semigroup homomorphism for which
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(\mathds{1}_{M})=\mathds{1}_{N}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
group
\emph default
 is a monoid 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 the operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{G}$
\end_inset

 of which (additionally) supports
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
inverse
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

elements 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists!a^{\prime}\in G$
\end_inset

 such that,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{G}a^{\prime}=a^{\prime}\bullet_{G}a=\mathds{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Exercise
The inverse of an element of a group is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime},a^{\prime\prime}\in G$
\end_inset

 be inverse elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
_
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The (unique) inverse element of 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in G$
\end_inset

 is commonly denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A subgroup of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $H\subseteq G$
\end_inset

 that is closed under the group operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{G}$
\end_inset

 and under the inverse 
\begin_inset Formula $\_^{-1}$
\end_inset

,
 meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in H$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{-1}\in H$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is a subgroup of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in H$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{G}b^{-1}\in H$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A group
\emph on
 homomorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to H$
\end_inset

 is a semigroup homomorphism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
For a group homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to H$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(\mathds{1}_{G})=\mathds{1}_{H}$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(a^{-1})=\varphi(a)^{-1}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
identity homomorphism
\emph default
 of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is the homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}:G\to G$
\end_inset

 that maps each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 to itself,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}a=a$
\end_inset

.
 An 
\emph on
inverse homomorphism
\emph default
 of a homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to H$
\end_inset

 is a homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi^{\prime}:H\to G$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varphi^{\prime}\circ\varphi:G\to G=\id_{G}\text{ and }\varphi\circ\varphi^{\prime}:H\to H=\id_{H}.
\]

\end_inset

Therefore an isomorphism (bijective homomorphism) is a homomorphism that has an inverse homomporhpism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
The set 
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi$
\end_inset

 of all automorphisms (homomorphism with an inverse) on a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

,
 form a group under the operation of function composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ:\varPhi\times\varPhi\to\varPhi$
\end_inset

,
 with identity element the identity homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}$
\end_inset

 and inverse homomorphisms with 
\begin_inset Formula $G=H$
\end_inset

 as inverse elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
_
\end_layout

